<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Martin Luther King Jr.</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1929">1929</a>–<a href="/years/1968">1968</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> King Jr. was an American Baptist minister and the best-known leader of the civil rights movement. Advocating non-violent protest, he led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montgomery-bus-boycott">Montgomery bus boycott</a> of 1955–1956 and campaigns in Birmingham and Selma, gave the I Have a Dream speech at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/march-on-washington">March on Washington</a> in 1963 and received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> in 1964; he was assassinated in Memphis in 1968.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/martin-luther-king-jr.jpg" alt="Martin Luther King Jr." width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yoichi Okamoto · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Martin_Luther_King,_Jr._and_Lyndon_Johnson_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The civil rights movement, with leaders such as <b>Martin Luther King Jr.</b>, won the Civil Rights Act of 1964 and the Voting Rights Act of 1965, ending legal segregation and restoring Black voting rights in the South.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-states">United States · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Martin Luther King Jr.</b> spoke of a “beloved community” of the reconciled, and in his “Letter from Birmingham Jail” (1963) he defended breaking unjust laws openly and accepting the penalty, and rebuked white moderates who preferred order to justice.</blockquote><a href="/movement/civil-rights-movement">Civil rights movement · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>Gandhi’s methods of non-violent resistance were studied by the leaders of the civil rights movement in the United States, among them <b>Martin Luther King Jr.</b>, who visited India in 1959, and by opponents of apartheid in South Africa, where Gandhi had first developed them.</blockquote><a href="/movement/indian-independence-movement">Indian independence movement · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Joan Baez led a crowd in it at the March on Washington in 1963, and more than 50,000 people sang it at <b>Martin Luther King Jr.</b>'s funeral.</blockquote><a href="/essays/how-songs-travel">How Songs Travel</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Martin Luther King Jr.</b> used its words in his speeches, among them &quot;How Long, Not Long&quot; at the Alabama State Capitol on 25 March 1965, at the end of the Selma to Montgomery march, and his last sermon in Memphis on 3 April 1968, the night before he…</blockquote><a href="/songs/battle-hymn-of-the-republic">Battle Hymn of the Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>On 28 August 1963 <b>Martin Luther King Jr.</b> recited its first verse near the end of his &quot;I Have a Dream&quot; speech at the March on Washington for Jobs and Freedom.</blockquote><a href="/songs/my-country-tis-of-thee">My Country, 'Tis of Thee</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Atlanta, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in St. Joseph's Hospital, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.75,-84.39&amp;year=1954" aria-label="Atlanta on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2397 940 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Martin Luther King Jr."><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2847 1210h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2847 1210h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.75,-84.39&amp;year=1954">What was here in 1954? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
